{{also|monaþ-}}
==Old English==

===Etymology 1===
From {{w|West Germanic}} ''*mānōþ'', from {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*mēnōþs}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|mōnath}} ({{cog|fy|moanne}}), {{cog|osx|mānuth}} ({{cog|nds|Maand}}), {{cog|odt|*mānoþ}} ({{cog|nl|maand}}), {{cog|goh|mānōd}} ({{cog|de|Monat}}), {{cog|non|mánaðr}} ({{cog|sv|månad}}), {{cog|got|𐌼𐌴𐌽𐍉𐌸𐍃}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|ang|mōnað}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|ang|/ˈmoːnɑθ/}}

====Noun====
{{ang-noun|head=mōnaþ|m}}

# [[month]]
#: {{ux|ang|Ūre ċild is būtan eahta '''mōnaþa''' eald.|Our baby is only eight '''months''' old.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Iċ forġeat þæt iċ hīe lætestne '''mōnaþ''' ġemētte.|I forgot that I met her last '''month'''.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Seofon '''mōnaþum''' lator iċ wæs of cwearterne.|Seven '''months''' later, I was out of jail.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Cum þū eft on six '''mōnaþum''' ġif þū þā ġīet leofast.|Come back in six '''months''' if you're still alive by then.}}
#: {{ux|ang|Hēo gǣþ ūt mid him þrīm oþþe fēower sīðum on '''mōnaþ'''.|She goes out with him three or four times a '''month'''.}}

